<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Age of Liberty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a>–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Age of Liberty was the period in Swedish history from 1718 to 1772 when royal power was sharply limited and Parliament (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a>) dominated government. It followed the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> and the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s great-power era. It ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III</a> restored stronger royal authority in a coup in 1772.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The shift of power from the monarch to parliament was a direct result of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> had sought to partition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> was killed at the siege of Fredriksten Fortress, Sweden made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and Denmark in 1719 and 1720 and with Russia by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721, giving up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and other lands. His sister Ulrika Eleonora, elected queen after his death, abdicated early in 1720 in favour of her husband, who was elected king as Frederick I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Under the new constitution all power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> and its four estates of nobles, priests, burgesses and peasants, which sat apart, and no measure became law without the assent of three of them. A secret committee of 50 nobles, 25 priests, 25 burgesses and only exceptionally 25 peasants held supreme executive, judicial and legislative power while the Riksdag sat: it prepared all bills, made and unmade ministries and ran foreign policy. In the recess the Privy Council governed, answerable to the Riksdag alone, and the king only chaired it, with two votes and the casting vote. Suffrage was not universal: the taxed peasantry had little weight, and commoners without taxed property had no vote at all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Politics came to turn on two parties. The Hats, named after the officers' three-cornered hat, sought to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> as a great power with French money and drove the cautious Count Arvid Horn from office at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> of 1738. Their war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, declared on 20 July 1741, lost most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, and the Treaty of Åbo of 7 May 1743 recovered most of it, at the price of electing Adolph Frederick of Holstein heir to the crown. Their entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> cost Sweden about 40,000 men.</p><p>The Caps, who took power in 1765, uncovered fraud in the Hat government's finances, cut the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a> and in 1766 issued a press ordinance that abolished prior censorship and opened public documents to inspection. They also drew close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which saw the existing constitution as the swiftest means of subverting Swedish independence. After the December Crisis of 1768 the Hats, again funded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> of 1769, but their promise to strengthen the monarchy came to nothing until the coup of 1772.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Age of Liberty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Charles XII’s death the <b>Age of Liberty</b> (1719–1772) gave power to the Riksdag and its parties, the Hats and the Caps, until Gustav III’s coup of 1772.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Age of Liberty</b> that followed Charles XII's death (1718–1772), the Riksdag effectively governed Sweden and the monarchy was reduced to a largely ceremonial role.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
